{"causes":[
{"cause":"The quota gate read below 50% but three back-to-back fleet strikes inside one 90-minute run pushed the weekly window to 70%+, locking the operator out of interactive Claude for the rest of the week; the pre-strike helper only samples, it cannot reserve.","earliest_signal":"status file shows quota.after - quota.before > 5 points on consecutive strikes","prevention":"section 3.4 reserve (strikes start only below threshold minus reserve) plus per-step reconciliation and overshoot flag; operator can lower --quota-threshold","interrogation_question":"What is the largest single-strike spend observed on this repo, and is the 5-point reserve larger than it?"},
{"cause":"fleet's implicit resume (reconcileRun) refused after a quota pause because the integration branch had been pruned by a human worktree cleanup, so every paused run became blocked and the queue starved.","earliest_signal":"reason resume-refused appearing more than once per day in the digest","prevention":"section 2.1 treats resume-refused as blocked with the branch preserved; the digest surfaces it; never silently restart","interrogation_question":"Who else runs git worktree prune on this checkout, and does fleet's status survive it?"},
{"cause":"The shaping model produced plausible scope globs that matched zero files on base, the bootstrap exception admitted them as a new package, and the worker spent 90 minutes creating a package nobody asked for.","earliest_signal":"triage PROCEED with a scope whose every glob resolves to zero existing paths","prevention":"section 5.3 bootstrap exception is limited to category feature and one new top-level dir that the ticket also registers; coldstart gap check","interrogation_question":"Should the bootstrap exception require an explicit adlc:begin block from a maintainer rather than a shaped scope?"},
{"cause":"CI's non-required rails-guard job went red on every autopilot PR because the added ticket shard's rails froze a path another in-flight human PR touched, and the autopilot kept spending CI fix rounds on a failure it could not fix.","earliest_signal":"rails-guard red with a path outside the PR's own diff","prevention":"section 6.9 CI fix rounds are capped at 2 and the failing job name is posted; rails are narrow by section 4.3 shaping","interrogation_question":"Should a rails-guard red caused by a path outside the PR diff skip the fix rounds and go straight to ci-red?"},
{"cause":"The bounded model plane hid the operator's ~/.npmrc and git credential helper, so the worker's npm ci inside its nested worktree failed on the first private-registry lookup and every run blocked at strike 1.","earliest_signal":"fleet gate.init exit != 0 with ENOTFOUND or E401 on the first live run","prevention":"R4 canary before enabling the service; READ_SET includes the pinned tool dirs; the R1 ticket's dry-run AC","interrogation_question":"Does this repo's install need any registry auth at all, and if so where must it live inside the synthetic home?"},
{"cause":"A human removed adlc:autopilot-blocked on an issue expecting a retry, but the unlabel actor check required admin/maintain and the operator's second account had only write, so nothing happened and the run sat quarantined for a week.","earliest_signal":"status unauthorized-unlabel entries accumulating","prevention":"section 2.1 re-applies the label and names the actor in status; digest comment explains the required permission","interrogation_question":"Is any collaborator without maintain permission expected to operate the autopilot's labels?"},
{"cause":"The rolling digest issue was closed by a bot, the autopilot created a new one every iteration because the cached number was stale and the label search returned nothing while the closed issue still held the label.","earliest_signal":"more than one open issue labeled adlc:autopilot-log","prevention":"section 10 re-validates the cached issue as OPEN, uses the lowest open number, reports digest-issue-ambiguous","interrogation_question":"Which bots or workflows in this repo close or relabel issues automatically?"},
{"cause":"The operator rebased main with a history rewrite; every open autopilot PR became DIRTY, the maintenance pass spent a conflict-fix round on each, and all five went stale in one iteration, exhausting the PR cap.","earliest_signal":"five stale labels applied within one iteration","prevention":"section 8 conflict-fix is one round then stale; stale PRs do not count toward the cap; the loop keeps selecting","interrogation_question":"Is main ever force-pushed, and should the autopilot pause maintenance when merge-base --all returns more than one base?"}
]}

